13. Human Creativity, Ownership, and Rights

TENTORA must create value for creatives rather than treat their work as unprotected input.

Every Ready Design must define:

original creator attribution;

source-design ownership;

permitted customization;

permitted AI-assisted changes;

usage license and output rights;

single-sale or repeat-license model;

creator compensation or revenue share; and

whether further changes require the original creator.

TENTORA must not use portfolios or Ready Designs for model training without explicit, informed permission and documented terms.

Creatives may generate revenue through template licenses, paid customization, commissioned work, and multidisciplinary projects.

14. Unique Value Proposition

TENTORA transforms fragmented creative discovery into connected, usable outcomes.

Its differentiation is based on seven principles:

Begin with the project sector and service—not an open profile directory.

Explain services through real creative work.

Offer creator-made Ready Designs for immediate needs.

Let clients describe what they need through a direct AI prompt.

Use one Main Taste Profile across one or multiple services.

Limit matching to a small, relevant set of creatives or teams.

Move from self-service to professional execution without restarting.

