21. Stage 1 Decisions and Exit Criteria

21.1 Approved product decisions

TENTORA uses five initial sectors to organize discovery.

The public experience offers Ready Designs, Create with AI, and Work With a Creative as direct entry points.

The underlying fulfillment model remains two connected paths: Design Now and Work With a Creative.

Ready Designs are primarily uploaded by approved creatives and studios, not anonymously presented as TENTORA-owned work.

Design Now begins with a structured Ready Design or an AI-assisted discovery result—not an unrestricted blank canvas.

The initial AI experience may retrieve and rank existing templates before full editable-design generation is introduced.

Every custom project uses one Main Taste Profile by default.

A selected service may have an optional taste refinement without repeating the full test.

Recommendations are limited to six creative, studio, or team options.

Self-service work can move into professional execution without restarting.

Creators retain attribution, ownership, permissions, and defined commercial rights.

AI supports intent understanding, discovery, customization, taste, matching, handoff, and coordination; it does not replace human authorship or professional craft.

21.2 Items requiring approval in Stage 2

Exact Phase 1 MVP scope and explicit exclusions.

Whether Phase 1 launches all five sectors or pilots Restaurants & Cafés first.

Pilot services and Ready Design formats.

Definition and limits of Create with AI in the MVP.

Creator onboarding, review, pricing, revenue share, and licensing model.

Named stakeholders and role capacity.

Deliverables and acceptance criteria.

Validated success metrics.

Delivery schedule and budget boundaries.

Governance, escalation, and decision rights.

Legal entity and intellectual-property structure.

21.3 Exit criterion

Stage 1 is complete when the project sponsor and core team accept this product definition and authorize Stage 2 to convert it into a bounded Project Charter.

